# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 has "forgo" and "hero_sacrifices → for_prized_end"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 has "◇forgo" and "hero_sacrifices" and "hero_sacrifices → for_prized_end"; they differ when only "people do without happiness" is true, which the first allows; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 has "◇forgo" and "hero_sacrifices → for_prized_end"
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S2 is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 with no modality; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "people do without happiness" is true and "the hero or martyr voluntarily gives up his own happiness", "the hero or martyr gives up his happiness for the sake of something he prizes more than his individual happiness", "possibly, people do without happiness" and "possibly, people do without happiness" are false. Is S2, "Unquestionably it is possible to do without happiness; it is done involuntarily by nineteen-twentieths of mankind, even in those parts of our present world which are least deep in barbarism; and it often has to be done voluntarily by the hero or the martyr, for the sake of something which he prizes more than his individual happiness.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 has "□(hero_sacrifices → for_prized_end)"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 with no modality; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 as what is necessary or possible: its "must" does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2, to: Suppose "necessarily, (if the hero or martyr voluntarily gives up his own happiness, then the hero or martyr gives up his happiness for the sake of something he prizes more than his individual happiness)" is false. Is S4, "It is noble to be capable of resigning entirely one's own portion of happiness, or chances of it: but, after all, this self-sacrifice must be for some end; it is not its own end; and if we are told that its end is not happiness, but virtue, which is better than happiness, I ask, would the sacrifice be made if the hero or martyr did not believe that it would earn for others immunity from similar sacrifices?", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 has "hero_sacrifices → believes_benefit"; they differ when only "the hero or martyr voluntarily gives up his own happiness" is true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "the hero or martyr voluntarily gives up his own happiness" is true and "the hero or martyr believes the sacrifice will earn for others immunity from similar sacrifices" is false. Is S5, "Would it be made, if he thought that his renunciation of happiness for himself would produce no fruit for any of his fellow creatures, but to make their lot like his, and place them also in the condition of persons who have renounced happiness?", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 have "◇renounce_other"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S7 is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 with no modality; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "may" or "can" or "should" does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "possibly, men renounce the personal enjoyment of life for a purpose other than increasing the happiness in the world" and "possibly, men renounce the personal enjoyment of life for a purpose other than increasing the happiness in the world" are false. Is S7, "He may be an inspiriting proof of what men can do, but assuredly not an example of what they should.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 have "¬admirable_other → ¬O renounce_other"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 has "◇renounce_other" and "¬admirable_other → ¬O renounce_other"; they differ when only "one who renounces the enjoyment of life for a purpose other than increasing the happiness in the world deserves admiration" is true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 has "¬O renounce_other"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 have "◇renounce_other ∧ ¬O renounce_other"; they differ when only "it ought to be that men renounce the personal enjoyment of life for a purpose other than increasing the happiness in the world" is true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: the conclusion is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 as what ought to be or is permitted; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 as both: its "may" or "can" or "should" does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "it ought to be that men renounce the personal enjoyment of life for a purpose other than increasing the happiness in the world" and "it ought to be that men renounce the personal enjoyment of life for a purpose other than increasing the happiness in the world" are true and "possibly, men renounce the personal enjoyment of life for a purpose other than increasing the happiness in the world", "possibly, men renounce the personal enjoyment of life for a purpose other than increasing the happiness in the world" and "it ought to be that men renounce the personal enjoyment of life for a purpose other than increasing the happiness in the world" are false. Is the conclusion, "He may be an inspiriting proof of what men can do, but assuredly not an example of what they should.", true then?
